response:
 Here we have $x-y=6$, and $x^2+y^2=24$. Both equations are already known, and we want to find the value of $x^3-y^3$. The problem basically gives us the information in this form. <answer> So first we need to find out the value of $x+y$. Can you figure out variables of $x$ and $y$ in the given equations? <answer> \begin{align*} (x-y)^2&=(6)^2\\x^2+y^2-2xy&=36\\24-2xy&=36\\-2xy&=12\\xy&=-6\\(x+y)^2 &= x^2+y^2+2xy=24+2(-6)=12\\x+y&=\pm 2\sqrt{3}\end{align*}
We are left with two possible values of $x+y$. <answer> To get the value of $x^3-y^3$, we have $$x^3-y^3 = (x+y)(x^2+y^2-xy)\\ = (x+y)(24-xy)\\\text{If } x+y=2\sqrt{3},\;\text{then x^3-y^3} = 12(24+6)=360.\\\text{If }x+y=-2\sqrt{3},\;\text{then }x^3-y^3=-12(24+6) = -360.$$$$\textbf{So } \boxed{|x^3-y^3|=360}.$$$$\end{align*}

Multiplying both sides of $x^2+y^2 = 24$ by $x-y$, we get $(x-y)(x^2+y^2) = 24(x-y)$. Expanding the left-hand side and simplifying with the given $x-y = 6,$ we get $x^3-y^3-xy^2+x^2y=24x-24y$. Factoring out $x^2+y^2$ from the LHS and one $12$ from the RHS, we get $x^2+y^2-xy = 12$. Hence $x^3-y^3 = 24.12 = 288.$ Finally, we take $|288 - 360| = 360.$$

