 Here we have $f(x)$ and $f(\cfrac{1}{x})$ so we should try to find $f(\cfrac{1}{x})$ i.e. evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$ with $\cfrac{1}{x}$ instead of $x$. So, , 
\begin{align*}
3f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)+\dfrac{2f(x)}{x}&=x^2  \\ \implies 3f\left(\dfrac{1}{x}\right)+2f(x)&=x^2 \cdot x & (\times x)&\\
\implies 3f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) + 2x &= x^3 \\ \implies f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) &= \dfrac{x^3 - 2x}{3}  
\end{align*}
Next, we share the denominator and subtract $f(x)$ from each side .Then we have $$3f\left(\cfrac{1}{x}\right)-f(x)+2f(x)=x^3\implies 3f\left(\cfrac{1}{x}\right)=x^3-f(x)$$ Finally, we add $f(x)$ to both sides and then multiply by three and simplify to get $f(x)$.
\begin{align*}
3f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)+2f(x)&=x^3\\
\implies 2f(x)&=\frac{x^3-\left(3f\left(\cfrac{1}{x}\right)\right)}{3}\\ \implies f(x)&=\dfrac{\frac{x^3-\left(3f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)\right)}{3}}{2} \\  \implies f(x)&=\dfrac{x^3-3f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)}{3\cdot 2}
